### A.2 Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Existence of Ghost Collapse)

**Proposition 3.1.** Under (P1)–(P2) and (F1), for every \(C^\dagger \in \mathbb{R}^m\) and every \(\epsilon > 0\), there exist initial conditions and a control \(u\) such that the solution of (2.1) satisfies \(\|C_t - C^\dagger\| < \epsilon\) and \(R_t < R_{\text{crit}}\) for all \(t \in [T, T + \Delta]\), where \(\Delta = \Theta(1/\epsilon)\).

*Proof.* We construct the trajectory explicitly.

**Step 1: Choose the fast subsystem equilibrium.** Fix \(R_0 < R_{\text{crit}}\) and choose \((\gamma, \lambda)\) such that the total potential \(V_{\text{total}}(\cdot, R_0; w, \gamma, \lambda)\) has a global minimizer \(C^*(R_0)\) in an \(\epsilon/2\)-neighborhood of \(C^\dagger\). Such a choice exists by (P1) (coercivity ensures a minimizer exists) and by the continuity of the minimizer in the parameters (which follows from the implicit function theorem under strict convexity, guaranteed by (7.1) at \(R_0\) if \(\gamma + \lambda\alpha_\Psi > |m(R_0)|_-\); if this inequality fails, choose \(\gamma\) large enough to satisfy it).

**Step 2: Initialize in the basin of attraction.** Choose \(C_0\) in the basin of attraction of \(C^*(R_0)\) for the fast subsystem, with \(\|C_0 - C^\dagger\| < \epsilon/2\). By (F1), the fast subsystem is exponentially attracting with rate \(\lambda_{\text{att}}(R_0) > 0\), so there exists \(T_1 = O(\log(1/\epsilon)/\lambda_{\text{att}}(R_0))\) such that \(\|C_t - C^*(R_0)\| < \epsilon/2\) for all \(t \geq T_1\).

**Step 3: Keep \(R\) below threshold.** Set \(u \equiv 0\) and choose \(R_0\) such that \(R_{\text{crit}} - R_0 = \delta_R > 0\). Under (3.2) with \(u = 0\), the slow variable evolves as

\[
R_t = R_0 + \epsilon \int_0^t f_R(C_s, R_s)\,ds + \sqrt{\epsilon}\sigma_R W_R(t).
\]

For \(t \leq T_2 = \delta_R/(2\epsilon \|f_R\|_\infty)\), the drift contribution is at most \(\delta_R/2\). The noise contribution is \(O(\sqrt{\epsilon}\sqrt{T_2}) = O(\sqrt{\delta_R})\), which can be made smaller than \(\delta_R/4\) by choosing \(\delta_R\) small enough (or by choosing a particular realization of \(W_R\) on a set of positive probability). On this event, \(R_t \leq R_0 + 3\delta_R/4 < R_{\text{crit}}\) for all \(t \leq T_2\).

**Step 4: Conclude.** Set \(T = T_1\) and \(\Delta = T_2 - T_1\). For \(t \in [T, T+\Delta]\), we have \(\|C_t - C^\dagger\| \leq \|C_t - C^*(R_0)\| + \|C^*(R_0) - C^\dagger\| < \epsilon\) and \(R_t < R_{\text{crit}}\). Since \(\Delta = \Theta(1/\epsilon)\), the ghost-collapse regime persists for a time of order \(1/\epsilon\). \(\square\)

