### A.5 Proof of Proposition 4.1 (Slow-Manifold Likelihood Consistency)

**Proposition 4.1.** Under the assumptions of Theorem 4.1, and assuming observations on a time grid of step \(\Delta t \ll 1/\epsilon\), the reduced likelihood \(\hat{p}(y_{1:N} \mid \theta)\) satisfies

\[
d_{\text{Hellinger}}\left(\hat{p}(\cdot \mid \theta), p(\cdot \mid \theta)\right) = O(\epsilon^{1/2})
\]

uniformly for \(\theta\) in a compact set, provided the true trajectory remains in \(R \geq R_{\text{crit}} + \delta\).

*Proof.* Let \(P_\theta\) and \(\hat{P}_\theta\) be the laws of the observation sequences under the full and reduced dynamics respectively. By Theorem 4.1, the reduced and full trajectories agree to \(O(\epsilon^{1/2})\) uniformly on \([0, T]\) with probability \(1 - O(\exp(-c/\epsilon))\). The observation maps differ by the same order in the observation noise variance. The Hellinger distance between two Gaussian distributions with means differing by \(\delta\mu\) and common covariance \(\Sigma\) is bounded by \(\|\Sigma^{-1/2}\delta\mu\|/2\), which is \(O(\epsilon^{1/2})\). On the exceptional set of probability \(O(\exp(-c/\epsilon))\), the Hellinger distance is bounded by 1, so its contribution to the total is \(O(\exp(-c/\epsilon)) = O(\epsilon^{1/2})\). Combining gives the result. \(\square\)

