### A.6 Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Convergence of Generator Estimation)

**Theorem 4.2.** Under (i) density of the dictionary in \(L^2(\mu)\) in the graph norm of \(\mathcal{L}\), (ii) ergodicity of the sampling, and (iii) \(\rho \to 0\) slower than \(n^{-1/2}\), the estimated generator \(\mathbf{L}\) converges to the projection of \(\mathcal{L}\) onto the dictionary span, with rate \(R_{\text{dict}} + O(n^{-1/2})\).

*Proof.* The proof combines three ingredients.

**Step 1: Galerkin projection error.** Let \(\Pi_J\) be the orthogonal projection onto \(\text{span}\{\psi_j\}_{j=1}^J\) in \(L^2(\mu)\). The Galerkin approximation \(\mathcal{L}_J = \Pi_J \mathcal{L} \Pi_J\) satisfies \(\|\mathcal{L} - \mathcal{L}_J\|_{L^2(\mu)} \leq R_{\text{dict}}\) by definition of the dictionary residual.

**Step 2: Finite-sample estimation error.** The regularized least-squares estimator \(\mathbf{K}\) converges to the true discrete-time Koopman operator restricted to the dictionary span at rate \(O(n^{-1/2})\) in the Frobenius norm, by standard results for regularized regression with ergodic sampling (e.g., Koltchinskii–Lounici).

**Step 3: Logarithm regularization error.** The regularized logarithm \(\text{Log}_\rho\) is Lipschitz on the spectrum of \(\mathbf{K}\) away from the branch cut, with Lipschitz constant \(1/\rho\). Choosing \(\rho \to 0\) slower than \(n^{-1/2}\) ensures that the logarithm error is dominated by the estimation error. Combining the three errors gives the stated rate. \(\square\)

