### A.11 Proof of Theorem 7.1 (Generalized Maakoul Principle)

**Theorem 7.1.** Assume (A1)–(A4), (P1)–(P2), (N1)–(N3), (S1)–(S2). Let \(w^*\) be a rest point of the replicator dynamics satisfying Theorem 6.1, and assume \(w_t\) is \(C^1\) with \(\dot{w}_t \to 0\). Fix \(R \geq R_{\text{crit}} + \delta\). If \(\gamma + \lambda\alpha_\Psi > |m(R)|_-\), then (i)–(v) hold.

*Proof.*

**(i) Existence.** The potential \(V_{\text{total}} = V_{\text{phys}} + \frac{\gamma}{2}\|C\|^2 + \lambda\Psi\) is continuous and coercive: the quadratic term \(\frac{\gamma}{2}\|C\|^2\) grows quadratically, and by (N1) \(\Psi\) grows at least as fast as \(\|C\|^2\) in the relevant directions (or is bounded below and coercive). A continuous coercive function on \(\mathbb{R}^m\) attains a global minimum.

**(ii) Uniqueness.** The Hessian is

\[
\nabla_{CC}^2 V_{\text{total}} = \nabla_{CC}^2 V_{\text{phys}} + \gamma I + \lambda\nabla_{CC}^2 \Psi \succeq (m(R) + \gamma + \lambda\alpha_\Psi)I.
\]

The condition \(\gamma + \lambda\alpha_\Psi > |m(R)|_-\) ensures \(m(R) + \gamma + \lambda\alpha_\Psi > 0\), so the Hessian is positive definite. Strong convexity implies uniqueness.

**(iii) Global exponential convergence.** Along the gradient flow \(\dot{C} = -\nabla V_{\text{total}}(C)\),

\[
\frac{d}{dt}\|C_t - C^*\|^2 = -2(C_t - C^*)^\top \nabla V_{\text{total}}(C_t).
\]

By strong convexity with modulus \(\delta(R) = m(R) + \gamma + \lambda\alpha_\Psi\),

\[
(C_t - C^*)^\top \nabla V_{\text{total}}(C_t) \geq \delta(R)\|C_t - C^*\|^2.
\]

Therefore \(\frac{d}{dt}\|C_t - C^*\|^2 \leq -2\delta(R)\|C_t - C^*\|^2\), giving (7.3).

**(iv) Continuous dependence.** The minimizer satisfies \(\nabla_C V_{\text{total}}(C^*, R; w^*) = 0\). The Hessian \(\nabla_{CC}^2 V_{\text{total}}\) is invertible by (ii). By the implicit function theorem, \(C^*\) is a \(C^1\) function of \((R, w^*)\), provided \(\Psi\) is \(C^1\) in \(w\) (assumption (N3)).

**(v) Collapse of the rate.** If \(m(R) \to -\infty\) as \(R \downarrow R_{\text{crit}}\), then \(\delta(R) = m(R) + \gamma + \lambda\alpha_\Psi \to -\infty\) unless \(\gamma\) and \(\lambda\) are increased. If \(m(R) \to 0^-\), then \(\delta(R) \to \gamma + \lambda\alpha_\Psi > 0\), but the margin above zero shrinks as \(R \downarrow R_{\text{crit}}\), so the damping required to maintain a fixed rate grows without bound. \(\square\)

