### A.13 Proof of Proposition 8.1 (Observability Rank Condition)

**Proposition 8.1.** Consider Model A with observations \(y_n = h(C_{t_n}) + \eta_n\). The slow variable \(R\) is locally weakly observable at \((C_0, R_0)\) if the observability codistribution

\[
\mathcal{O} = \text{span}\{d(\mathcal{L}_f^k h_j) : k \geq 0, j = 1, \dots, q\}
\]

has rank \(k\) on a neighborhood of \((C_0, R_0)\).

*Proof.* This is the standard observability rank condition for nonlinear systems (Hermann–Krener). The Lie derivatives \(\mathcal{L}_f^k h_j\) are the repeated derivatives of the observation along the drift vector field \(f = (f_C, \epsilon f_R)\). Local weak observability holds if and only if the differentials of these functions span the cotangent space at \((C_0, R_0)\). In the scalar case \(m = k = 1\) with \(h(C) = C\), the first Lie derivative is \(\mathcal{L}_f h = f_C(C, R)\), so \(d(\mathcal{L}_f h) = \partial_C f_C\, dC + \partial_R f_C\, dR\). The rank condition requires \(\partial_R f_C \neq 0\) (together with \(\partial_C f_C \neq 0\), which is generically satisfied). Persistence of excitation ensures that the trajectory does not remain on a set where \(\partial_R f_C = 0\). \(\square\)

---

### A.14 Proof of Proposition 8.2 (Direct Observability)

**Proposition 8.2.** Under Model B with \(\partial_R h \neq 0\) and bounded observation noise, \(R\) is directly observable.

*Proof.* The observation map \(h(C, R)\) has \(\partial_R h \neq 0\), so the Jacobian of \(h\) with respect to \(R\) has full column rank. By the implicit function theorem, \(R\) can be locally recovered from the observation \(y = h(C, R)\) given \(C\). With bounded noise, the recovery is Lipschitz in the noise level. The observability rank condition is satisfied trivially because \(d h\) already contains the \(dR\) component. \(\square\)

