## Appendix F: Glossary of Terms

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| **Attractor** | A set of states toward which a dynamical system tends to evolve; a local minimum of a potential function. |
| **Bifurcation** | A qualitative change in the structure of a dynamical system as a parameter varies. |
| **Coercive function** | A function \(f\) such that \(f(x) \to +\infty\) as \(\|x\| \to \infty\). |
| **Collapse** | An irreversible phase transition in which the prior attractor landscape is destroyed. |
| **Debris mapping** | The iterative identification of surviving state variables (functional and regulatory) after collapse. |
| **Fold bifurcation** | A saddle-node bifurcation where two equilibria coalesce and disappear, rendering the prior attractor inaccessible. |
| **Generator** | The infinitesimal generator of a Markov semigroup; for diffusions, a second-order differential operator. |
| **Ghost collapse** | A pathological equilibrium where functional capacity has returned but regulatory integrity remains below the critical threshold. |
| **Gradient system** | A dynamical system \(\dot{x} = -\nabla V(x)\) that follows the steepest descent of a potential. |
| **Hysteresis** | The phenomenon where the recovery threshold exceeds the collapse threshold, so that marginal increases in the control parameter do not produce marginal increases in recovery probability. |
| **Koopman operator** | The operator that advances observables along the flow of a dynamical system. |
| **Kramers' escape time** | The mean first passage time for a stochastic system to leave a potential well. |
| **Kuramoto model** | A model for synchronization of coupled oscillators. |
| **Linear Illusion** | The systematic misinterpretation of functional output as a proxy for genuine system health when regulatory integrity has collapsed. |
| **Maakoul's Theorem** | The theorem stating that damping and narrative penalty together create a unique global attractor even when the physical potential is non-coercive. |
| **Narrative field** | The weighted combination of actor narrative kernels that encodes the meaning-structure maintained by collective agency. |
| **Narrative penalty** | A convex function added to the potential to encode meaning constraints. |
| **Potential game** | A game whose payoff vector is the gradient of a potential function; replicator dynamics converge to Nash equilibria. |
| **Recovery latency** | The inverse of the smallest eigenvalue of the linearized fast-subsystem matrix; diverges as regulatory integrity approaches the critical threshold. |
| **Regulatory integrity** | The structural condition of a system's self-regulating mechanisms; the slow variable \(R\). |
| **Replicator dynamics** | The evolutionary dynamics \(\dot{w}_i = w_i(\pi_i - \bar{\pi})\) for the evolution of strategy weights. |
| **Restoration Fallacy** | The mistaken belief that a collapsed system can return to its pre-collapse state. |
| **Rosen contraction** | A condition on the Jacobian of a game (symmetric negative definite) that guarantees convergence of the replicator dynamics. |
| **Second Orbit** | A new stable configuration that emerges from the debris of collapse under new constraints. |
| **Slow manifold** | An invariant manifold to which the fast dynamics relax on a short timescale, on which the slow dynamics evolves. |
| **Strong convexity** | A function \(f\) is strongly convex with modulus \(\alpha\) if \(\nabla^2 f \succeq \alpha I\). |
| **Structural hysteresis** | The regime \(R_{\text{crit}} \leq R < R_{\text{recovery}}\) in which marginal increases in \(R\) do not produce marginal increases in recovery probability. |
| **Structural potential** | The potential \(V_{\text{phys}}\) encoding the constraints imposed by surviving debris and the new environment. |

